1. Problem Statement

To develop an AVL Tree Traversal System using C++. 
The system constructs a self-balancing binary search tree from user-entered integers. 
It displays inorder, preorder, postorder, and level-order traversals with the balance factor beside each node.
The system also allows users to insert additional values dynamically, updates the traversals, \
and verifies whether the inorder traversal is sorted.

2. Approach

The program uses an AVL tree, which is a self-balancing binary search tree. 
Each node stores an integer, its height, and pointers to its left and right children.
When a value is inserted, the program follows binary search tree rules.
It updates the height and balance factor of affected nodes and performs LL, RR, LR, or RL rotations whenever necessary.
The program implements the following features:
Balance Factor Display: Displays each node with its balance factor using the format Data(BF=value)
Additional Insertion: Allows users to insert new integers through a menu and automatically updates the tree and traversals.
Inorder Traversal: Visits the left subtree, root, and right subtree.
Preorder Traversal: Visits the root, left subtree, and right subtree.
Postorder Traversal: Visits the left subtree, right subtree, and root.
Level-order Traversal: Visits nodes level by level using a queue.
Sorted-Order Verification: Checks whether the inorder traversal is in strictly ascending order.
Duplicate values are ignored to maintain unique keys in the AVL tree.

 Complexity Analysis

Let n be the number of nodes in the AVL tree.

Operation                      Time Complexity

Insertion of one value                 O(log n)

Initial tree construction              O(n log n)

Inorder traversal                      O(n)

Preorder traversal                      O(n)

Postorder traversal                     O(n)

Level-order traversal                   O(n)

Sorted-order verification               O(n)

Space complexity                        O(n)

4.Conclusion

The modified AVL Tree Traversal System successfully demonstrates the implementation of a self-balancing binary search tree
in C++. It allows users to insert initial and additional integers, display four types of traversals with balance factors, 
and verify the sorted order of the inorder traversal. The project improves understanding of AVL rotations, 
tree balancing, recursion, queues, and efficient data organization.
